# Everything in this repo's module graph that is derived from the pinned
# Clockwork drop, quarantined in one file with one job.
#
# Why it has to exist at all: Bazel honours `bazel_dep` overrides only in the
# root module. The drop declares every one of its dependencies as a versionless
# `bazel_dep` and satisfies each one with an override, so when the drop is a
# *dependency* rather than the root, none of those `bazel_dep`s resolve. The
# whole table has to be restated here -- all of it, not the subset our targets
# reach, because every entry is versionless and therefore unresolvable on its
# own. The drop's toolchain registration is root-only for the same reason
# (theirs is marked as a dev dependency, which is dropped for consumers).
#
# Immediate provenance: this is a verbatim copy of rusty-cogs'
# bazel/clockwork_overrides.MODULE.bazel at rev 4e8f8e8 -- the revision
# MODULE.bazel pins rusty-cogs at -- itself a mirror of the drop's own table.
# rusty-cogs is a *dependency* of this repo, so its overrides do not reach us
# either -- the copy is the only way to hold the same graph. On a drop upgrade,
# upgrade rusty-cogs first and re-copy this file from it, rather than
# re-deriving from the drop twice.
#
# Derivation rule: this file mirrors the drop's own MODULE.bazel dependency
# table, deliberately keeping its list-and-comprehension shape so that a drop
# upgrade is a mechanical diff of the two tables rather than a re-reading of
# both. On upgrade: bump the `archive_override` below, diff this table against
# the new drop's, apply, rebuild. Three intentional differences from the drop's
# text, and no others:
#
#   1. Patch labels point at our own verbatim copies of the drop's patch files,
#      under bazel/clockwork-patches/. Bazel accepts a patch label only from the
#      main repository, so referencing them in place is not possible.
#   2. The drop's `local_path_override` branch is absent. Its table currently
#      has no entry using it, and an entry that appeared would point into the
#      drop's own working tree, which we do not have. One showing up on upgrade
#      is a decision, not a transcription.
#   3. `platforms` is pinned at 1.1.0 rather than the drop's 1.0.0. rules_rust
#      names `@platforms//cpu:loongarch64` in its target-triple table, and 1.0.0
#      does not declare that constraint value, so every `select()` over a Rust
#      platform fails to resolve and no Rust target in this repo analyzes. 1.1.0
#      is the first release that declares it and is otherwise a superset. This
#      one is ours, not the drop's, and it reverts the moment the drop moves its
#      own pin forward.
#
# Standing consequence of consuming the drop this way: the pinned versions
# below bind *our* whole module graph, not just the drop's subtree. Any rule set
# added to the root module has to be compatible with this set, and a conflict
# shows up at module resolution.
#
# TODO(override-regen): the diff is done by hand; a script should regenerate this
# segment from a drop's MODULE.bazel and the gate should check the result.

bazel_dep(name = "clockwork")

archive_override(
    module_name = "clockwork",
    # The pin: a commit tarball plus its content hash. Upgrading the drop is
    # editing these three lines, deliberately, on a branch of its own.
    # `strip_prefix` is load-bearing -- GitHub nests a commit archive under a
    # `<repo>-<commit>/` directory, and Bazel finds no MODULE.bazel without it.
    integrity = "sha256-KCFSCAJZDUr60nH/vZX+AxG/gAEh8lH5ro5I8s0J+Ds=",
    strip_prefix = "clockwork-ece332ad111805e3b2a355244835b87f073ee65e",
    urls = ["https://github.com/stackav-oss/clockwork/archive/ece332ad111805e3b2a355244835b87f073ee65e.tar.gz"],
)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Mirrored from the drop's MODULE.bazel
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

